ation of the interdisciplinary nature of exercise physiology.","lo_domain_description":"","offerings":[],"lo_domain":[],"order":"1","number":"1","lo_cl_id":"de5c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013101","code":"CLO1","linking_id":"ec7482ebdb759850eb6b147a3a961999","cl_id":"de5c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013101","ga_map":[]},{"academic_item":{"value":"HESC1501","cl_id":"525ce027c3920390a9607d2a050131fd","key":"code"},"description":"Apply basic principles of movement analysis","lo_domain_description":"","offerings":[],"lo_domain":[],"order":"4","number":"4","lo_cl_id":"d25c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013102","code":"CLO4","linking_id":"247482ebdb759850eb6b147a3a9619bb","cl_id":"d25c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013102","ga_map":[]},{"academic_item":{"value":"HESC1501","cl_id":"525ce027c3920390a9607d2a050131fd","key":"code"},"description":"Describe the processes involved in patient care, from initial patient referral through to exercise programming and delivery.","lo_domain_description":"","offerings":[],"lo_domain":[],"order":"3","number":"3","lo_cl_id":"d65c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013102","code":"CLO3","linking_id":"687482ebdb759850eb6b147a3a96199f","cl_id":"d65c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013102","ga_map":[]}],"enrolment_rules":[{"active":"true","type":{"label":"Enrolment Requirements","value":"info"},"order":"","cl_id":"225c2427c3920390a9607d2a0501310c","academic_item":{"value":"HESC1501","cl_id":"525ce027c3920390a9607d2a050131fd","key":"code"},"description":"Prerequisite: 3871 program<br/><br/>"}],"faculty_detail":[{"parent":{"value":"","cl_id":null,"key":"name"},"ext_id":"MED","cl_id":"7c2459ef1bbae810d044ffbbdc4bcbd4","code":"MED","description":"Translating discoveries into breakthrough cures, therapies into treatment strategies, students into professionals improving healthcare globally.","published_in_handbook":{"label":"Yes","value":"1"},"name":"Faculty of Medicine and Health","active":"true","name_no_url":"","url":"https://www.unsw.edu.au/medicine-health","name_url":"Faculty of Medicine and Health"}],"study_level_single":{"label":"Undergraduate","value":"ugrd"},"code":"HESC1501","attributes":[{"name":"Introductory Course","code":"introductory_course","description":"This course is usually taken in the early part of a program and may be required as a pre-requisite before taking a more advanced course."}],"timetableURL":"http://timetable.unsw.edu.au/2027/HESC1501.html","subject_sample_text_staticcontent":"","credit_points_header":"6 Units of Credit","description":"<p>This course presents an overview of the vocational activities within the allied health profession of Exercise Physiology. The sub-disciplines of exercise science (exercise physiology, biomechanics, motor control and exercise psychology), and related biomedical sciences, are introduced through examining how the body responds to physical activity. This course includes a clinical practicum component in the University&#39;s Lifestyle Clinic. This course is restricted to students enrolled in the Bachelor of Exercise Physiology 3871.</p>","grading_basis":"Standard UNSW grades","academic_item_type":"Course","course_outline_url":"https://www.unsw.edu.au/course-outlines/course-outline#courseCode=HESC1501&year=2027","no_enrolment":"false","offering_detail":{"key":"ugrd","fees":{},"offering_terms":"","fees_table":"","erg":[],"campus":{"name":""}},"eqivalents":[],"exclusion":[],"search_title":"HESC1501 - Introductory Exercise Science","additional_information":"","study_level_tooltip":"Undergraduate: A level of study that leads to the award of a Diploma, Advanced Diploma, Associate Degree or a Bachelor Degree (pass or honours).","course_outline_label":"Visit Course Outline website","campus":"Sydney","academic_calendar_tooltip":"Consists of three standard terms, each with ten weeks teaching, and an optional five week summer term.  Some programs in Medicine, AGSM, and UNSW Global have different term dates.  See the university website for details.
","grading_basis_tooltip":"Students will receive a mark out of 100 and a grade: HD, DN, CR, PS or FL. The result from this course is used in the calculation of the student's WAM.","hb_assessments":[{"delivery_variation":{"cl_id":"5a5ce027c3920390a9607d2a050131fe","name":"In-person - Standard (usually weekly or fortnightly)"},"assessments":[{"assessment_name":"SYNOPSIS LABORATORY REPORT","applies_to_all_delivery_variations":"true","assessment_id":"2","weighting":"15","individual":{"label":null,"value":null},"assessment_type":{"label":"Report","value":"report"},"description":"<p>Student will independently <strong>summarise the purpose, methods, results and conclusions</strong> from the laboratory conducted on Reaction time and motor learning. They will be required to include with this summary a reference to a relevant journal article.</p>","learning_outcomes":[{"value":"CLO5","cl_id":"d65c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013101","key":"code"}],"delivery_variations":[],"cl_id":"125c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013103","links":[]},{"assessment_name":"ONLINE QUIZZES","applies_to_all_delivery_variations":"true","assessment_id":"1","weighting":"10","individual":{"label":null,"value":null},"assessment_type":{"label":"Examination","value":"examination"},"description":"<p>There will be 5 online quizzes throughout the term. Completing each online activity will earn 2% of the course grade, for a total of 10% for completing all tasks. Marks are awarded once you have attained 80% correct on the quiz, however you can do the quiz as many times as you wish. The online activities are designed to reinforce concepts and skills covered in the lectures. </p>","learning_outcomes":[{"value":"CLO3","cl_id":"d65c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013102","key":"code"},{"value":"CLO2","cl_id":"da5c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013101","key":"code"},{"value":"CLO1","cl_id":"de5c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013101","key":"code"}],"delivery_variations":[],"cl_id":"d65c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013104","links":[]},{"assessment_name":"END OF SESSION EXAMINATION","applies_to_all_delivery_variations":"true","assessment_id":"4","weighting":"50","individual":{"label":null,"value":null},"assessment_type":{"label":"Examination","value":"examination"},"description":"<p>The purpose of the exam is to test understanding of the concepts covered in the ENTIRE COURSE. The format will be multiple choice, short answer and long answer questions. The exam will be held during the end of session exam period.</p>

<p> </p>","learning_outcomes":[{"value":"CLO4","cl_id":"d25c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013102","key":"code"},{"value":"CLO5","cl_id":"d65c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013101","key":"code"},{"value":"CLO3","cl_id":"d65c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013102","key":"code"},{"value":"CLO2","cl_id":"da5c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013101","key":"code"},{"value":"CLO1","cl_id":"de5c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013101","key":"code"}],"delivery_variations":[],"cl_id":"2e5c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013106","links":[]},{"assessment_name":"CLINICAL EXPERIENCE REPORT","applies_to_all_delivery_variations":"true","assessment_id":"3","weighting":"25","individual":{"label":null,"value":null},"assessment_type":{"label":"Report","value":"report"},"description":"<p>Students will report on the clinical activities observed during visits to an exercise physiology clinic (clinical sessions are conducted in the UNSW Medicine Lifestyle Clinic). Students will briefly and clearly report on their clinical observation experience including the process of identifying initial medical conditions, key data from initial assessments, design of exercise programming, movement analysis and professional conduct considerations (in line with the Exercise and Sports Science Australia (ESSA) Code of Conduct)). They will also be required to locate and summarise a journal article that addresses the scientific basis of exercise prescription for a selected clinical condition.</p>","learning_outcomes":[{"value":"CLO4","cl_id":"d25c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013102","key":"code"},{"value":"CLO5","cl_id":"d65c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013101","key":"code"},{"value":"CLO3","cl_id":"d65c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013102","key":"code"}],"delivery_variations":[],"cl_id":"ea5c2427c3920390a9607d2a05013109","links":[]}]}],"inactive_course_alert":null,"notes":"","links":[]}

